          ],
          "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.085,
            0.642,
            0.107
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.1.2 Red rose as an indicator"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.113,
            0.883,
            0.153
          ],
          "content": "You might have observed many coloured flowers in your surroundings. Try making your indicators using these flowers."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.066,
            0.222,
            0.253,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.084,
                0.417,
                0.24,
                0.463
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.6: Red rose petals immersed in hot water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.166,
            0.545,
            0.187
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 2.3: Let us prepare"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.201,
            0.619,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": "Collect some fallen petals of red roses available in your surroundings (Fig.  2.5). It is advised not to pluck flowers. You may pick petals or flowers fallen on the ground."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.316,
            0.617,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "Take a fistful of the collected petals of red roses and wash them with water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.378,
            0.617,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "Crush the petals using a mortar and pestle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.42,
            0.593,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "Place them in a glass tumbler."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.63,
            0.184,
            0.886,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.687,
                0.383,
                0.835,
                0.401
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.5: Red roses"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.445,
            0.883,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "Pour some hot water into the glass tumbler to ensure that the crushed flower petals are completely immersed."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.218,
            0.482,
            0.258,
            0.51
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.486,
            0.82,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "**Caution** — Perform this step under the supervision of an adult."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.507,
            0.883,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": "Cover the glass tumbler with a lid. Wait for 5 – 10 minutes till the water becomes coloured (Fig. 2.6), and filter it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.548,
            0.883,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "The filtrate (liquid after filtration) is the required flower extract (Fig. 2.7) to be used as an acid-base indicator."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.295,
            0.594,
            0.849,
            0.787
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.342,
                0.794,
                0.734,
                0.812
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.7: Test tube containing the red rose extract"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.829,
            0.545,
            0.85
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 2.4: Let us fi nd out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.865,
            0.884,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": "Place 10 – 20 drops of the prepared red rose extract in each of two small transparent bottles or test tubes. Mark them A and B."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.018,
